 ## Overview
-Parabolic Ray Tracing is a method for computing light ray trajectories in media with spatially varying Refractive Index Fields (RIF) by approximating the RIF such that ray paths are second-order polynomials within tetrahedral cells [S1]. It solves the problem of exact intersection computation with cell boundaries, which is difficult when ray trajectories involve transcendental functions, by reducing the intersection problem to solving quadratic equations [S1]. It differs from linear ray tracing methods by accounting for continuous curvature within cells, and from general non-linear solvers by providing closed-form analytical solutions for the trajectory [S1].
+Parabolic Ray Tracing computes light ray trajectories in media with spatially varying Refractive Index Fields (RIF) by modeling the squared RIF as a linear function within tetrahedral cells, yielding second-order polynomial ray paths [S1]. It solves the problem of exact intersection computation with cell boundaries, which is otherwise difficult when trajectories involve transcendental functions, by reducing boundary crossings to solving quadratic equations [S1]. It differs from linear ray tracing by accounting for continuous curvature within cells, and from general non-linear solvers by providing closed-form analytical solutions for the trajectory [S1].
 
